<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">1st Czechoslovak Army Corps</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as 1st Czechoslovak Army Corps in the USSR</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1st Czechoslovak Army Corps was a Czechoslovak formation that fought alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, formed in 1944 from the brigade raised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludvik-svoboda">Ludvík Svoboda</a>. It suffered heavy losses in the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dukla-pass">Dukla Pass</a> in autumn 1944 and fought through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> until 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/1st-czechoslovak-army-corps.jpg" alt="1st Czechoslovak Army Corps" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ThecentreCZ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_1st_Czechoslovak_Army_Corps_in_USSR_(reverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The corps grew out of the 1st Czechoslovak Independent Field Battalion, formed at Buzuluk in the Urals as the first foreign unit to fight beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>. Its men were former Czechoslovak legionaries, refugees, Slovak prisoners of war and defectors, and Volhynian Czechs, and Lieutenant-Colonel Ludvík Svoboda took command on 15 July 1942. The government-in-exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> wanted the unit kept for the liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, but its officers wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> and were sent into action. At Sokolovo, part of the Third Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a>, in March 1943, the battalion lacked anti-tank weapons and suffered heavy losses against German armour.</p><p>In May 1943 it was enlarged into the 1st Czechoslovak Independent Brigade, reinforced largely by Rusyn and Ukrainian prisoners released from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>. The brigade, 3,348 strong, was among the first troops to reach the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> in 1943.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>As the Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic was retaken, about 12,000 Volhynian Czechs enlisted, and the corps was created on 10 April 1944 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernivtsi">Chernivtsi</a>, with three and later four infantry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> and tank, artillery and engineer units. Serving first with the 38th Army of the 1st Ukrainian Front, it entered the East Carpathian offensive in September 1944 and fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dukla-pass">Dukla Pass</a>, losing 1,630 dead and 4,069 wounded of 14,900 men. During the battle Marshal Konev replaced its first commander, Jan Kratochvíl, with Svoboda. The 2nd Parachute Brigade and a fighter regiment were sent behind the lines to support the Slovak National Uprising, and after its defeat the paratroopers fought on as partisans in the Slovak mountains.</p><p>From November 1944 the corps served with the 4th Ukrainian Front, taking part in the liberation of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, while its tank brigade, air division and some infantry fought in the Moravian-Ostrava Operation. Svoboda became Minister of Defence in the government appointed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosice">Košice</a>, and Karel Klapálek succeeded him. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> offensive of 6 to 11 May 1945 the corps, by then 48,400 strong, lost 112 killed and 421 wounded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The corps ceased to exist soon after the war. Under the provisional organisation approved on 25 May 1945 it became the 1st Czechoslovak Army, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> becoming divisions, and the Volhynian Czechs who had joined it formed the core of the post-war Czechoslovak Army.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “1st Czechoslovak Army Corps in the Soviet Union” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Second World War Czechoslovak airmen flew with the Royal Air Force, a battalion fought at Tobruk, and the <b>1st Czechoslovak Army Corps</b> under Ludvík Svoboda fought alongside the Red Army, including at the Dukla Pass in 1944.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
